course: everyday
lesson: 267
title: Traffic lights, crossings and road signs
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: At a pedestrian crossing a red standing figure means wait, even if the road looks empty, because the cars have a green light and may come fast.
explain: Crossings are made for everyone: a beeping or ticking sound tells blind people when to cross, and in the UK a little cone under the button box spins for people who can neither see nor hear well.
explain: Road signs and markings are a language: red circles usually give orders or say no, give way or yield means let others go first, and lines on the road show where cars stop and where they may not park.
explain: A girl in Edinburgh presses the crossing button once and waits; her friend presses it ten times, but the lights change at the same moment for both.
fact: When the red standing figure shows, you wait on the pavement even if the road looks empty, because the cars have a green light.
fact: You press the crossing button once and wait; pressing many times does not make it faster.
fact: The button tells the lights that someone is waiting to cross.
fact: A beeping or ticking sound at a crossing tells blind people when it is safe to cross.
fact: In the UK a small cone under the crossing box spins when it is safe to cross, to help deafblind people.
fact: A give way or yield sign means drivers must slow down and let other traffic go first.
fact: A school zone sign means cars must drive slower near the school at certain times, because children are near the road.
fact: A no pedestrians sign means nobody may walk there; you never walk on a motorway, freeway or highway, where cars drive very fast.
fact: Signs with a red circle usually give orders or say no.
fact: A stop line shows where drivers must stop, and double yellow lines in the UK and Ireland mean no parking.
fact: The USA and the UK use miles per hour; Canada, Australia, New Zealand and Ireland use kilometres per hour.
fact: The same number on a speed sign means a faster speed in miles per hour than in kilometres per hour.
fact: A signpost arrow shows the way to a town and the number shows how far it is; in the UK that number is in miles.
exercise: The red standing figure is showing but the road looks empty. What do you do? | Wait on the pavement until the green figure shows | Cross quickly, because no car is coming | Cross if your friends cross first | Walk out slowly so cars can see you
exercise: Why do you wait at a red figure even if the road looks empty? | The cars have a green light and may come fast | The red figure is only a suggestion for small children | The lights will break if you cross | Police are always watching
exercise: How should you use the push button at a crossing? | Press it once and wait for the green | Press it many times to make it faster | Hold it down until the lights change to green | Press it only if a car is coming towards you
exercise: What does the push button at a crossing do? | It tells the lights that someone is waiting | It stops all the cars at once, wherever they are | It makes a sound for the drivers | It turns all the lights green
exercise: Why does a crossing beep or tick? | To tell blind people when it is safe to cross | To warn drivers that they should speed up | To tell people the time of day | To show that the lights are broken and need a repair
exercise: In the UK, what does the small cone under the crossing box do? | It spins when it is safe to cross, for those who cannot see or hear | It counts how many people have crossed | It tells you how fast the cars are going along the road towards the crossing | It is a button to call the police
exercise: What does a give way or yield sign tell drivers? | Slow down and let other traffic go first | Drive faster to clear the junction | Stop forever until the police come | Children may play on this road
exercise: What does a school zone sign mean? | Cars must drive slower near the school at certain times | Children may cross anywhere without looking | Only school buses may drive there | Parking is free for parents all day long outside the school gates
exercise: You see a no pedestrians sign at the start of a freeway. What does it mean? | Nobody may walk there, because cars go very fast | Walkers must keep to the left side of the road | You may walk there only in daylight | Only children may not walk there, adults are allowed to
exercise: What do red circle signs usually do? | Give orders or say no | Give directions to towns | Show where the toilets are | Tell you about places to visit
exercise: What do double yellow lines at the edge of a road mean in the UK? | Cars may not park there | Children may cross there | Cars must drive in the middle | Buses must stop there to let people off
exercise: What does a white stop line across the road show? | Where drivers must stop at the junction | Where people should stand to wait | Where a bike lane begins | Where the speed limit ends
exercise: Which countries use miles per hour on their speed signs? | The USA and the UK | Canada and Australia | New Zealand and Ireland | Canada and New Zealand
exercise: A signpost says "Bath 12" with an arrow pointing left. What does it tell you? | Bath is 12 miles away along the road to the left | Bath has 12 roads going to the left | You may drive at 12 miles an hour all the way to Bath | Bath is 12 minutes away to the right
test: A boy in Brisbane sees his friend press the crossing button again and again. What should he tell him? | Once is enough; the lights know someone is waiting | Keep pressing, or the lights will forget you | Press it harder so it works faster and the cars stop sooner | Stop pressing, it only works for adults
test: A girl hears a crossing start to beep fast. What does it mean? | The green figure is on and it is safe to cross | The lights are broken and need fixing | A fire engine is coming down the road | The cars have just got a green light
test: A speed sign in Canada says 50 and one in the USA says 50. Which is true? | The US limit is faster, because it is in miles per hour | They are exactly the same speed | The Canadian limit is faster | Speed signs mean the same everywhere in the world, whatever the country
test: Which sign is most likely to forbid something? | A sign with a red circle on it | A sign with an arrow to a nearby town | A sign showing a picture of a bed | A sign with the name of a street
test: Your car stops at a give way sign while a lorry passes. Why? | The sign means let other traffic go first | The lorry driver asked to go first by flashing his lights | Give way signs mean the road is closed | The car has run out of petrol
test: Near a school at 3 o'clock, why do cars drive slower? | A school zone lowers the speed limit while children are near | Cars always slow down when it is afternoon | Drivers like to look at the school | The road is always narrower near schools, so cars must slow down for it
test: A family's car breaks down on a freeway in Texas. Why should the children not walk along it? | Cars go very fast there and walking is not allowed | It is too far to the next town | The road is too hot for shoes | They might get lost among the many signs along the freeway
test: A man parks on double yellow lines in London. Which is true? | He has parked where parking is not allowed | Double yellow lines mean parking for two cars | He is in a place kept for buses only | Double yellow lines mean slow down
test: Why is it important that crossings have sounds and spinning cones? | So blind and deafblind people can cross safely too | So children have something fun to play with | So drivers know when to speed up | So the lights work in the dark
test: red man showing but the road's empty, can i go | No, wait for the green, cars have a green light | Yes, if it's totally empty both ways | Yes, if you run | Only if a grown-up goes
test: pressed the button like 10 times, it's still red | Press once and wait, more presses don't help | Press it 20 more times | Hold it down for a full minute till it changes | Go anyway, it's broken
test: sign says 30 in the uk n 30 in australia, same speed? | No, 30 mph is faster than 30 km/h | Yes, exactly the same | 30 km/h is faster | Signs mean the same everywhere, numbers don't change
test: road's a shortcut but it's a motorway, can we walk along the side | No, never walk on a motorway | Yes, on the edge | Yes, if you're quick and wear something bright | Only in the daytime
explain: A boy in Texas started crossing when the "Don't Walk" sign was already flashing; a turning truck did not see him, and he was hit and badly hurt.
explain: A girl in Sydney crossed against a red man because the road looked empty; a car came fast round the bend on its green and struck her, and she was in hospital for weeks.
fact: Crossing against the signal is one of the main ways children are hit by cars at junctions.
fact: Drivers turning at corners are watching other cars and may not see a child on the crossing.
fact: If the green figure starts flashing while you are already crossing, keep walking steadily to the other side; do not run back or stop in the middle.
test: red man but no cars, can i just go | Wait for the green man; cars can come fast | Go, the road's empty | Run, then it's safe | Go if your mate goes first and says it's clear
test: Why is a turning truck so dangerous for someone on the crossing? | The driver may not see you beside or in front of it | Trucks always stop for walkers | Trucks turn very slowly | The driver can see everyone from up high
test: flashing don't walk, i'm already halfway over | Keep going steadily to the other side | Turn round and run back to where you started | Stop and wait in the middle | Stand still and wave at the cars
test: did u hear some kid crossed on the red man cos the road looked empty and a car came round the corner super fast? real lesson? | Wait on the red figure even if the road looks empty | Cross on red if you run | Look once and go on red | Red only matters when there are cars you can actually see
test: apparently if u press the crossing button 10 times it goes green straight away. probably just a rumour? | Yes; press once and wait | No, more presses mean faster | No, it goes red for you instead | No, it only works with 10 presses
test: my mate says 30 on a speed sign is the same speed everywhere. right? | No, 30 mph is faster than 30 km/h | Yes, a number is a number | Yes, all countries use miles | No, kilometres are faster than miles, so 30 km/h wins
exam: Identify the meaning of a sign bearing a red circle. | It usually gives an order or a prohibition | It indicates a place of interest | It shows the distance to a town | It indicates a picnic area or a place where one may rest
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the crossing button? | It informs the lights that someone is waiting; one press suffices | It changes the lights at once if pressed repeatedly | It switches off the traffic lights | It summons a police officer
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the spinning cone beneath a UK crossing box. | It spins when it is safe, to assist deafblind persons | It is a decoration without function | It shows the speed of cars | It spins when cars approach, warning pedestrians to stay back
exam: Ascertain the meaning of a school zone sign. | Cars must drive more slowly near the school at certain times | Children may play in the road there | Parking is free outside the school | Buses may not stop there
